use std
into pimage.amni

This file is a module with no main (namespace pimage). It finds the images in a PDF and writes each one to its own file so that OCR or a vision model can read it: JPEG data is written as it is (`.jpg`), JPEG 2000 data as it is (`.jp2`), and every other image is decoded and written as a binary PNM file (`.pgm` for gray, `.ppm` for RGB, `.pbm` for 1-bit gray). Every module-level name starts with `pi_` or `PI_`. PDF objects are read only through the `pdf` module: `pdf::pf_open(data) bool`, `pdf::pf_pages` (a list of `pdf::PfPage`, whose `res` is the page's resources dict node, -1 when none), `pdf::pf_nodes` (a list of `pdf::PfNode` with fields `t`, `a`, `b`), `pdf::pf_kids` (a list of ints), `pdf::pf_kind(n)` (a `pdf::PF_` type constant or -1), `pdf::pf_dget(n, key)` (the dereferenced value node of a dict or stream for a key written without its slash, or -1), `pdf::pf_name_is(n, name)`, `pdf::pf_bytes(n)` (the bytes of a name or string node), `pdf::pf_int(n, default)`, `pdf::pf_real(n, default)`, `pdf::pf_len(n)` and `pdf::pf_at(n, i)` (the length of an array node and its dereferenced element i), `pdf::pf_deref(n)`, and `pdf::pf_decode(n, out) bool` (the decoded data of a stream; it stops at DCTDecode, JPXDecode, CCITTFaxDecode and JBIG2Decode, returning false with the bytes decoded so far in out, and when the stream's last filter is DCTDecode or JPXDecode those bytes are the complete JPEG or JPEG 2000 file). A dict node n has `pdf::pf_nodes[n].a` as the first slot of its pairs in `pdf::pf_kids` and `pdf::pf_nodes[n].b` as the number of pairs, the key node of pair i being `pdf::pf_kids[a + 2 * i]` and its value node (not yet dereferenced) `pdf::pf_kids[a + 2 * i + 1]`. A stream node s has `pdf::pf_nodes[s].b` as its index in `pdf::pf_streams`, which identifies the stream object.

```amni
import os;
import dm;
import pdf;
```

Image kinds are module constants: `PI_JPG = 1`, `PI_JP2 = 2`, `PI_PGM = 3`, `PI_PPM = 4`, `PI_PBM = 5`. `PI_MIN = 32` is the smallest width and height an image may have to be kept. `PI_DEPTH = 12` is the deepest nesting of form XObjects. `PI_MAXPX = 100000000` is the most pixels one image may have.

Module state: `var pi_ipage int[>..]` (the page number from 1 of each written image), `var pi_ipool byte[>..]` with `var pi_iat int[>..]` and `var pi_ilen int[>..]` (the path of each written image), `var pi_iw int[>..]`, `var pi_ih int[>..]` (its size in pixels), `var pi_ik int[>..]` (its kind), `var pi_skip = 0` (images skipped), `var pi_seen int[>..]` (the stream indexes already visited, images and forms alike), `var pi_dir byte[>..]` (the output directory, with a slash at the end unless empty), `var pi_buf byte[>..]` (decoded image data), `var pi_s byte[>..]` (one 8-bit value per sample), `var pi_out byte[>..]` (a PNM file being built), `var pi_pal byte[>..]` (an Indexed color space's lookup table), `var pi_idx = false` (the color space just resolved is Indexed), `var pi_base = 0` (an Indexed space's base component count), `var pi_hival = 0` (its highest index), `var pi_tmp byte[>..]`.

`pi_reset()` clears every list above, sets `pi_skip` to 0, `pi_idx` to false, and `pi_base` and `pi_hival` to 0.

## Results

`pi_count() int` is the number of images written. `pi_page(i int) int` is the page number (from 1) image i was first found on, `pi_path(i int) const byte[:]` its file path (a slice of `pi_ipool`), `pi_width(i int) int` and `pi_height(i int) int` its size in pixels, `pi_kind(i int) int` its kind; each gives 0 (the path an empty slice) for an index that is out of range. `pi_kind_name(k int) const byte[:]` is "jpg", "jp2", "pgm", "ppm" or "pbm" for the kinds 1 to 5 and "" for anything else. `pi_skipped() int` is the number of images skipped.

`pi_num(v int, digits int, out byte[>..]&)` appends the decimal digits of the non-negative v padded with leading zeros to at least `digits` digits.

## Decoding

`pi_filter(v int) int` classifies image stream v by the last filter of its /Filter (a name, or the last element of an array; 0 when there is none): 2 for DCTDecode or DCT, 3 for JPXDecode, 4 for CCITTFaxDecode or CCF, 5 for JBIG2Decode, 1 for anything else (including no filter).

`pi_space(cs int, depth int) int` resolves the color space node cs and returns its number of components (1, 3 or 4), or 0 when it is missing, nested more than 3 deep or not supported; it sets `pi_idx` to false first. A name: DeviceGray, G and CalGray give 1; DeviceRGB, RGB and CalRGB give 3; DeviceCMYK and CMYK give 4; any other name gives 0. An array is by its first element (a name): CalGray gives 1 and CalRGB gives 3; ICCBased gives the /N of its second element (a stream) when that is 1, 3 or 4 and otherwise 0; Indexed or I resolves its second element with `pi_space` at depth plus 1 as the base (it must give 1, 3 or 4 and not be Indexed itself, otherwise return 0), reads its third element as the highest index (an int clamped to 0 through 255) into `pi_hival`, stores the base's component count in `pi_base`, loads its fourth element into `pi_pal` (cleared first: for a string node its bytes, for a stream node its decoded data through `pdf::pf_decode`), sets `pi_idx` to true and returns 1; every other array (Separation, DeviceN, Lab, Pattern) gives 0.

`pi_hdr(magic const byte[:], w int, h int, maxval int, out byte[>..]&)` clears out and appends a PNM header: the magic ("P4", "P5" or "P6"), a newline, the width, a space, the height, a newline and, when maxval is above 0, maxval and a newline.

`pi_raw(v int, w int, h int) int` converts the decoded sample data in `pi_buf` of the non-mask image stream v (w by h pixels) into a PNM file in `pi_out` and returns its kind (`PI_PGM`, `PI_PPM` or `PI_PBM`), or 0 when the image cannot be converted. Steps:
- The bits per component is /BitsPerComponent (default 8); it must be 1, 2, 4, 8 or 16, otherwise return 0. n is `pi_space` of the image's /ColorSpace, and 0 gives return 0. A row is (w times n times the bits per component, plus 7) divided by 8 bytes, and the image needs that times h bytes; when `pi_buf` is shorter than a quarter of the needed bytes return 0, and when it is shorter than the needed bytes append 0 bytes up to that length.
- The decode array is /Decode when it is an array with at least 2 times n numbers and the image is not Indexed (an Indexed image has none); it is "inverted" when it exists and its first number is above its second.
- A gray image with 1 bit per component that is not Indexed becomes a PBM: `pi_hdr` with "P4", width, height and maxval 0, then every byte of the needed data replaced by 255 minus itself (a PDF 1-bit gray 1 is white and a PBM 1 is black), or unchanged when the decode array is inverted; return `PI_PBM`.
- Otherwise unpack the needed data into `pi_s`, one byte per sample, w times n per row: with 8 bits the byte itself, with 16 bits the high byte of each 2-byte sample, with 1, 2 or 4 bits the sample read most significant bit first from the row (sample j is at bit j times the bits per component), scaled to 0 through 255 by multiplying by 255 and dividing by the largest sample value (2 to the power of the bits, minus 1), except that an Indexed image keeps the raw index. Then, when there is a decode array that is not exactly 0 and 1 for every component, replace each sample of component c (the sample number modulo n) by 255 times (d0 plus (d1 minus d0) times the sample divided by 255), rounded and clamped to 0 through 255, where d0 and d1 are the decode numbers 2c and 2c plus 1.
- Then by color: for 1 component not Indexed, `pi_hdr` "P5", width, height, 255 followed by `pi_s`; return `PI_PGM`. For 3 components "P6" followed by `pi_s`; return `PI_PPM`. For 4 components (CMYK) "P6" followed, for each pixel, by red, green and blue bytes where red is (255 minus cyan) times (255 minus black) divided by 255, green uses magenta and blue uses yellow in the same way (integer division); return `PI_PPM`. For an Indexed image look up each pixel's index (taken as at most `pi_hival`) in `pi_pal` as `pi_base` bytes starting at index times `pi_base` (bytes beyond the table count as 0): a gray base gives "P5" with one byte per pixel and returns `PI_PGM`; an RGB base gives "P6" with the 3 bytes and a CMYK base the converted 3 bytes, returning `PI_PPM`.
- For 8 bits per component, 1 or 3 components, no Indexed palette and no decode array, skip the unpacking: the data after the header is the first needed bytes of `pi_buf`.

## Walking

`pi_save(page int, w int, h int, kind int, data const byte[:]) bool` writes data to the next output file: the path is `pi_dir`, "img-", the number of images written so far as `pi_num` with 3 digits, a period and `pi_kind_name` of kind, written with `os::write_file`. When that fails return false. Otherwise append the path to `pi_ipool` (recording its start and length), push the page, width, height and kind on the lists, and return true.

`pi_image(v int, page int)` handles the image stream node v found on page number page: read its /Width and /Height (`pdf::pf_int`, default 0); return when /ImageMask is the bool true (the node's `a` is 1), or when either size is below `PI_MIN`; when w times h is above `PI_MAXPX` add 1 to `pi_skip` and return. By `pi_filter`: 4 and 5 (CCITTFax and JBIG2) add 1 to `pi_skip` and return; 2 (DCT): decode with `pdf::pf_decode` into `pi_buf` ignoring its result, and when the data starts with the bytes 255 and 216 save it as `PI_JPG` with `pi_save`, otherwise add 1 to `pi_skip`; 3 (JPX): the same, and when the data starts with the bytes 0, 0, 0, 12 or 255, 79 save it as `PI_JP2`, otherwise skip; anything else: `pdf::pf_decode` into `pi_buf` (when it returns false add 1 to `pi_skip` and return), then `pi_raw`: a kind of 0 adds 1 to `pi_skip`, otherwise `pi_out` is saved with that kind. A failed `pi_save` adds 1 to `pi_skip`.

`pi_res(res int, page int, depth int)` (a recursive function; the fallback for a page without content) visits the /XObject dict of the resources dict node res (return when res is -1 or the /XObject is not a dict): for each pair, up to 100000, dereference the value node with `pdf::pf_deref`; skip it unless it is a stream (`pdf::pf_kind` is `pdf::PF_STREAM`); skip it when its stream index (`pdf::pf_nodes[value].b`) is already in `pi_seen`, otherwise record it there. When its /Subtype is the name Image call `pi_image`. When it is the name Form and depth is below `PI_DEPTH`, call `pi_res` with the form's own /Resources (when it has none, with res), the same page and depth plus 1.

`pi_do(name const byte[:], res int, page int, depth int)` (a recursive function: it and `pi_scan` call each other) handles one XObject painted by a `Do` operator with the resources dict node res: return when res is -1 or its /XObject is not a dict; look name up in that dict with `pdf::pf_dget` (the key is the name without its slash); return unless the value is a stream (`pdf::pf_kind` is `pdf::PF_STREAM`) and skip it when its stream index (`pdf::pf_nodes[value].b`) is already in `pi_seen`, otherwise record it there. When its /Subtype is the name Image call `pi_image`. When it is the name Form and depth is below `PI_DEPTH`, decode it with `pdf::pf_decode` into a new local list and call `pi_scan` on those bytes with the form's own /Resources (when it has none, with res), the same page and depth plus 1.

`pi_scan(s const byte[:], res int, page int, depth int)` (a recursive function) scans the content stream bytes s, in order, for `name Do` operators and calls `pi_do` for each. Whitespace bytes are 0, 9, 10, 12, 13 and 32; delimiter bytes are the characters ( ) < > [ ] { } / and %; every other byte is regular. Walk with an index i from 0 and the last name as a start and a length (the length -1 when the previous token was not a name); every step advances i by at least 1. A whitespace byte is skipped. A percent sign skips to the end of its line (the next byte 10 or 13). An opening parenthesis skips a string to its matching closing parenthesis (nested pairs count, a backslash skips the byte after it) and clears the last name. A slash starts a name: it runs over the regular bytes after the slash and becomes the last name (a name of length 0 is allowed). Any other delimiter is skipped and clears the last name. A run of regular bytes is a token: when it is "Do" and the last name exists, call `pi_do` with that name; when it is "BI" (an inline image) skip ahead to just after the first "EI" that has a whitespace byte before it and a whitespace byte or the end after it, or to the end when there is none; every token clears the last name.

`pi_pagerun(p int, num int)` handles page index p, page number num: take the page's /Contents (`pdf::pf_dget` of `pdf::pf_pages[p].node`). When it is a stream, decode it with `pdf::pf_decode` into a local list; when it is an array (`pdf::PF_ARR`), for each element up to 10000 (`pdf::pf_at`) that is a stream decode it into a second local list and append it to the first followed by the byte 10. When the first list ends up empty, call `pi_res` with the page's resources, num and depth 0 (a page without any content falls back to its resource dict); otherwise call `pi_scan` on the list with the page's resources, num and depth 0.

`pi_extract(data const byte[:], dir const byte[:]) int` finds and writes the images of the PDF in data and returns the number written. Call `pi_reset()`; set `pi_dir` to dir with a slash added when it is not empty and does not already end with one; when dir is not empty and `os::is_dir` says it is not a directory, make it with `os::make_dir` (a failure is ignored). Open the PDF with `pdf::pf_open` (return 0 when that fails). For each page in `pdf::pf_pages`, in order, call `pi_pagerun` with its index and its page number counted from 1. When `pi_skip` is above 0 add the warning, through `dm::dm_warn_add`, of the count in decimal followed by " image(s) skipped (CCITTFax, JBIG2, undecodable or unsupported color space)". Return `pi_count()`.
